# REGRESSION FIXTURE — a `Set[String]` literal built from LIVE named locals must
# CLONE each element (the source stays readable) and must own what it stores.
#
# AXIS + CELL (Core #12): this is the LIVE-SOURCE cell of the set-literal
# element-ownership axis. Its sibling
# `attack_101_set_string_heap_elem_double_free.gg` is the DEAD-SOURCE cell
# (elements are temporaries from `mk(...)`, consumed straight into the literal).
# The two together cover both values of the source-liveness axis; neither alone
# does, and the live-source half is the one that was BLOCKED rather than merely
# untested — see below.
#
# ⚠⚠ THIS CELL COULD NOT BE MADE RED, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT (Core #12:
# "if neither form of red is possible, say so in the header and state what it
# pins instead"). FOUR deliberate breaks of the set-literal ownership path were
# tried at HEAD and this fixture stayed GREEN through every one:
#
#   1. `gorget_ordered_set_new_str` -> the byte-keyed `gorget_dict_new(...)`
#      (`src/backend/c/runtime/runtime_set.c`), i.e. `key_drop=NULL`. The
#      SIBLING fixture double-frees under ASan; this one does not, because a
#      byte-keyed set stores a shallow alias it never frees and `main` frees the
#      original exactly once — no double free, no leak.
#   2. `elem_mode` forced to `AssignMode::Move` in
#      `lower_set_literal_from_array`. No effect: the upstream
#      `materialize_for_slot` has already produced a fresh temp, so the move is
#      of the temp.
#   3. the clone-if-live `materialize_for_slot` deleted for elements 2..N.
#   4. breaks 2 and 3 together — a live named local consumed with no clone AND
#      forced Move. STILL green.
#
# The reason is `_new_str`'s `key_materialize`: the str-keyed map deep-copies
# the key inside `gorget_set_add`, which makes the CALLER-side ownership
# decision unobservable for this shape. So the caller-side clone-if-live picker
# is NOT what this fixture guards, and any comment claiming otherwise is rot.
#
# WHAT IT ACTUALLY PINS, and why it is still worth committing:
#   * THE SHAPE COMPILES AND RUNS AT ALL. At the base of the fix
#     `Set[String] s = {x, y}` from live locals was a hard "shallow copy of
#     resource" validator panic — the clone-if-live picker for set literals had
#     been REVERTED because a live element's clone leaked into the byte-variant
#     set. That is a genuine historical red; reproducing it needs a pre-fix
#     compiler, which is why it is recorded here rather than re-run.
#   * IT CLOSES THE SOURCE-LIVENESS AXIS, whose other value is the graduated
#     sibling `attack_101_set_string_heap_elem_double_free.gg` (dead-source
#     temporaries). Neither cell alone samples that axis.
#   * A LEAKED CLONE ON THIS PATH still reds it, because it is wired to
#     `security_safe_no_leak` (`detect_leaks=1`) rather than a stdout compare.
#     That channel is live even though the four breaks above do not reach it.
# Treat it as a BOUNDARY/CONTROL cell, not a mechanism guard.
#
# ⛔ WHAT IT DOES **NOT** PIN, though an earlier draft of this header claimed it
# twice: "the sources survive, so `print(x)` / `print(y)` fail loudly if the
# literal MOVED them". Break #4 above IS that case — a live named local consumed
# with no clone AND forced Move — and it stayed GREEN, rc 0, correct stdout,
# LSan-clean, reproduced independently. `key_materialize` deep-copies the key
# and the `MoveZero` is elided, so the source is never observably dead. The
# reads below are a CHEAP FORWARD HOPE, not a demonstrated guard: only a future
# change that makes the source observably dead would red them.
#
# ⚠ HEAP-FORCED ELEMENTS. `mk()` concatenates at runtime so each element is a
# real allocation; string LITERALS here would be a false negative, because a
# static key has nothing to clone, leak or double-free.
#
# ⚠ ELEMENTS ARE READ AFTER THE SET IS BUILT — keep the reads, but do not read
# them as coverage. See "WHAT IT DOES NOT PIN" above: break #4 moved a live
# named local out of this literal and `print(x)` / `print(y)` still printed
# correctly, so these reads are not a demonstrated move guard. They cost two
# lines and would catch a future change that makes the source observably dead,
# which is why they stay.
#
# Wired test: `set_string_live_source_elements_safe` (tests/security.rs), which
# runs it under `--sanitize` with `detect_leaks=1`, so a leaked clone is a hard
# failure and not just a stdout compare.
#
# INTENDED: aa / bb / aa / bb, exit 0, sanitizer-clean.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    String x = mk("a", "a")
    String y = mk("b", "b")
    Set[String] s = {x, y}
    for e in s:
        print(e)
    # Forward guard only — see the header: a forced MOVE of these locals was
    # measured NOT to change this output.
    print(x)
    print(y)
